# CANONICAL §7.3 fixture — `Hole.thread` (WP-T1, cosmetic tapped hole).
#
# WHAT IS PINNED HERE, AND WHAT DELIBERATELY IS NOT.
#
# No measured geometry number appears in this file (`signatures.geometry` is
# `$hex64`). Option B — `params.diameter` IS the drilled hole, threaded or
# not, and the worker reads NOTHING from `thread` for geometry — is proven
# numerically in worker/tests/test_hole_op.cpp
# (`test_threaded_hole_is_volume_identical_to_unthreaded`), which can measure
# a solid. Same contract/corpus split the OffsetFace pair and
# `analyze_edge_op_range` use.
#
# What IS pinned here is the protocol shape a Rust or frontend reader branches
# on: that a threaded Hole produces the IDENTICAL `bodyEvents`/`needsRepair`/
# `signatures` shape an unthreaded one does (ROUND 1 vs ROUND 2 below — the
# `expect` `payload` block is CHARACTER-FOR-CHARACTER the same object, proving
# `thread`'s presence changes nothing about the step's identity bookkeeping),
# that the two `detail` preflight refusals carry their reasonCode at
# `stage:"preflight"` (ROUND 3 `HOLE_THREAD_DETAIL_UNSUPPORTED`, ROUND 4
# `HOLE_THREAD_DETAIL_UNKNOWN`) without moving the head, and that the worker
# forward-ignores an unknown `thread` key and never range-checks a field it
# doesn't consume (ROUND 5 — `pitchMm:-1` plus an unknown `handedness` key
# still drills cleanly; the authoring-side refusal of that same `pitchMm:-1`
# lives in `record.rs`'s `hole_thread_validation_matrix`, a DIFFERENT trust
# boundary this fixture does not exercise).
#
# ROUND 1's Hole is DISCARDED (never committed) and ROUND 2's — byte-identical
# send params plus `thread` — is ACCEPTED, so ROUND 3/4/5 run against a host
# that already has ROUND 2's hole cut into its top face. ROUND 3/4 are
# preflight refusals (they never reach face resolution, so this is moot for
# them); ROUND 5 drills at a DIFFERENT point on the same face, because
# re-drilling ROUND 2's now-permanently-removed location would correctly
# refuse ("point lies outside the resolved face boundary") — a worker
# correctness fact discovered by running this fixture against the real
# worker, not a bug. ROUND 5's `expect` payload is still the exact ROUND 1
# shape (bodyEvents/needsRepair/signature format), which is the property
# under test.
